@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · restart dsh web, then add an account in Settings, Mail：Everything runs on your DSH host: mail is fetched straight from your own
servers into a local cache, nothing is sent anywhere else, and there is no
telemetry.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · Accounts：Open **Settings, Mail** (or the gear in the Mail tab), pick a type and fill in
the form. **Test connection** logs in (IMAP LIST, POP3 STAT, SMTP verify or an
API call) and reports each step; it never shows a secret.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · Accounts：**Agents** see an account only when `agent.allow` is on (new accounts start
off). `canRead`, `canSend` and `canMass` narrow it further, and
`agents.allowedAccounts` can restrict the list globally.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · Unsubscribe：```
unsub.example.org {
  @unsub path /dsh-mail-unsub/*
  handle @unsub { reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:<listenPort> }
  handle { respond 404 }

(behind a Cloudflare tunnel: an ingress rule for that hostname and path to
the same port). Keep the login on everything else.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · Upgrading from v0.1：```yaml
dsh-mail:
  defaultProvider: lettermint
  agents: { confirmMode: reply-only, firstContactConfirm: false }
  schemaVersion: 1

@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · Upgrading from v0.1：Behaviour change: queue entries in state `queued` (manual queue) are no longer
sent by the timer; they wait for **Send now** or **Approve** in the tab, as the
v0.1 documentation already described.

@lucagiftzek/dsh-mail README · Install：Install from npm: `dsh plugin --profile web add @lucagiftzek/dsh-mail`, or from GitHub: `dsh plugin --profile web add github:lucagiftzek/dsh-mail`.

